      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Main Content Body -->
  <div class="prose prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-6 mb-8">
    <h2>1. The Geometry Advantage: Single Slope vs. 4-Way Compound Envelope</h2>
    <p>
      In a conventional center-drain shower, the drain sits in the middle of the floor, requiring water to pitch inward from all four perimeter corners. Because a planar sheet of rigid porcelain tile cannot bend across compound curves, tile size in traditional center-drain pans is strictly limited to 2x2-inch or 3x3-inch mosaic tiles that conform to the bowl, or requires diagonal cross-cuts across 12x24 tiles.
    </p>
    <p>
      With a linear drain running edge-to-edge across one wall, the entire floor pitches in a single flat, planar direction. Because the surface is entirely flat:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No Diagonal Cuts:</strong> You can install 24x48-inch, 32x32-inch, or full porcelain slab panels without diagonal relief cuts.</li>
      <li><strong>Zero Water Pooling:</strong> Eliminates flat low-spots that frequently plague hand-floated four-way mud beds.</li>
      <li><strong>Clean Sightlines:</strong> Grout lines extend in uninterrupted parallel grids from the dry bathroom floor directly into the shower enclosure.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Subfloor Recessing &amp; Joist Modification Engineering</h2>
    <p>
      To achieve a true curbless, zero-threshold entry where the shower floor and bathroom floor meet on an identical level plane, the shower floor must start below the existing bathroom subfloor. The total thickness needed for the mortar slope bed, waterproofing membrane, thinset, and tile thickness ranges between 1-1/8 inches and 1-1/2 inches.
    </p>

    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-5 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 not-prose my-6">
      <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Structural Framing Strategies for Curbless Recessing</h3>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-xs">
        <div class="bg-white p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-200">
          <div class="font-bold text-gray-900 text-sm mb-1">Method A: Sistered 2x10s with Recessed 3/4" CDX</div>
          <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            Fasten new full-span 2x10 joists directly alongside existing joists using staggered 3-inch structural timber screws every 12 inches. Cut down the top edge of the original joists by 1-1/4 inches within the shower footprint. Install 2x4 ledger strips along the sides of the sistered joists and drop 3/4-inch CDX subfloor onto the ledgers.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-200">
          <div class="font-bold text-gray-900 text-sm mb-1">Method B: Engineered Pre-Sloped Foam Tray System</div>
          <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            Utilize pre-manufactured structural shower trays (e.g. Schluter-KERDI-SHOWER-TT or Wedi Fundo Riolito). These EPS foam cores taper from 1-1/8" down to 3/8" directly at the drain throat. They integrate an engineered factory-pressed bonded waterproof fleece, eliminating manual dry-pack screeding errors.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>3. The 1/4" Per Foot Pitch Formula and Drain Sizing Table</h2>
    <p>
      Per International Residential Code (IRC) Section P2709.3, shower pan linings must slope toward the drain at a minimum pitch of 1/4 inch per foot (1:48 or approximately 2% grade) and not more than 1/2 inch per foot (1:24). If the run from the entrance threshold to the rear linear drain is 4 feet (48 inches), the required total drop is exactly:
    </p>
    <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-600 p-4 font-mono text-sm text-blue-900">
      Total Drop = 4.0 feet x 0.25 inches/foot = 1.00 inch of total elevation fall
    </div>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 not-prose">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-xs border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
        <thead class="bg-gray-100 text-gray-700 uppercase font-semibold">
          <tr>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Shower Depth (Run to Drain)</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Minimum Elevation Drop (1/4"/ft)</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Maximum Allowable Drop (1/2"/ft)</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Linear Drain Length Recommendation</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
