            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">66" to 70" Channel</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>4. Mandatory 24-Hour Standing Water Flood Test</h2>
    <p>
      Under plumbing code IRC P2709.2, every curbless shower waterproof pan lining must undergo a <strong>24-hour hydrostatic flood test</strong> prior to setting tile. In curbless designs where there is no physical curb, insert an inflatable pneumatic test plug into the 2-inch drain waste pipe, build a temporary sandbag or 2x4 wrapped in plastic dam across the threshold, and fill the pan with water up to 2 inches depth.
    </p>
    <p>
      Mark the exact waterline with a pencil. Inspect the lower floor ceiling and joist cavities with an inspection mirror and moisture meter after 24 hours. A drop in water level beyond normal evaporation (1/16") indicates an active leak that must be repaired before closing the tile assembly.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Trade Field Note -->
  <FieldNote
    expertName="Elena Rostova, CBO"
    role="Certified Building Official & Material Takeoff Analyst"
    experience="18+ Years Field Experience"
    avatarBg="bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800"
    initials="ER"
    title="The Capillary Creep Threat: Why Pre-Sloped Under-Pan Waterproofing is Mandatory"
    note="A major code mistake DIYers make is floating a flat mortar bed directly on the recessed wood subfloor, painting liquid membrane over the top, and hoping the tile grout stops water. Grout is NOT waterproof! Water constantly permeates through cement grout lines and migrates downward into the thinset bed. If your thinset bed rests on a flat plane without fall, water sits trapped between the tile and membrane, growing black mold, creating sour sour-milk smells, and eventually degrading polymer bonds. The structural bed underneath your waterproof membrane MUST possess the exact same 1/4-inch per foot slope as the finished tile surface. Water that penetrates the tile must have a continuous gravity-fed escape route down into the drain weep holes or bonded flange."
  />

  <!-- Costly Mistakes -->
  <CostlyMistakes
    tradeName="Linear Shower Drain Waterproofing"
    mistakes={[
      {
        number: 1,
        title: "Using Incompatible Thinset to Bond Sheet Membranes",
        costImpact: "$5,800 (Debonded Tile Slabs & Hollow Crunching Sound)",
        description: "Applying heavy polymer-modified thinset between impervious sheet membranes (like KERDI) and impervious porcelain tile traps moisture that cannot evaporate, preventing the mortar from ever curing.",
        solution: "Strictly follow manufacturer specs: use unmodified dry-set mortar (ANSI A118.1) for fleece membranes requiring air hydration, or manufacturer-specific specialized hybrid adhesives."
      },
      {
        number: 2,
        title: "Sizing the Linear Drain Too Short for the Shower Width",
        costImpact: "$1,400 (Awkward Pitch Wing Cuts & Side Wall Flooding)",
        description: "Installing a 36-inch linear drain in a 48-inch wide shower creates 6-inch flat 'dead zones' in the corners where water pools, necessitating awkward compound pitch cuts.",
        solution: "Size the linear drain channel within 1 to 2 inches of the total wet wall width so the drain trough spans virtually the entire width of the sloped plane."
      },
      {
        number: 3,
        title: "Omitting the Mechanical Clamping Ring O-Ring Gasket",
        costImpact: "$3,600 (Subfloor Rot Directly Around Waste Pipe Penetration)",
        description: "Omitting the rubber O-ring or silicone bead between the 2-inch drain outlet and the ABS/PVC shower drain base creates a hidden drip leak beneath the pan.",
        solution: "Assemble the threaded clamping ring using the supplied EPDM rubber gasket and torque the stainless bolts in an alternating star pattern."
      }
    ]}
  />

